<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enmebaragesi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/28th-century-bce">28th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enmebaragesi was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> who lived around 2700 BCE and is one of the earliest Mesopotamian rulers attested in inscriptions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> records that he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a>, among the earliest recorded conflicts in the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enmebaragesi.jpg" alt="Enmebaragesi" width="320" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: पाटलिपुत्र · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mebaragsi,_King_of_Kish_(transcription_of_fragment,_original_in_Iraq_National_Museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Enmebaragesi was the penultimate king of the first dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> and, like his son and successor Aga, reigned at a time when Kish had hegemony over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. His name is first written Mebarasi, and the prefix En is an honorific title that was not part of the original name. Interpretations of the rest differ: it can be read as Priest who permeates the throne or as Crown fit for a ruler. He is the earliest ruler on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> whose name is attested directly from archaeology.</p><p>The dating of his reign has been debated, with proposals ranging from the beginning of Early Dynastic I (c. 2900 to c. 2800 BCE) to Early Dynastic IIIa (c. 2600 BCE). Most scholars favour c. 2600 BCE, while others place the inscriptions slightly earlier, at c. 2700 BCE. Four inscriptions carry the name, but only one gives the title king: a vessel fragment of unknown provenance, in the Baghdad Museum, reading Mebarasi, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>. Gianni Marchesi and Niccolò Marchetti propose that the other three refer to a non-royal person of the same name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> gives him a reign of 900 years and records him as the one who carried away the weapons of the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>. It credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> with hegemony over northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> and Eresh, and large parts of the Diyala Region. A later tradition of Kishite expansion into Susiana and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> appears in an inscription of the Early Dynastic II king Enna-il. The Tummal Inscription, an Old Babylonian tablet, says that Enmebaragesi built the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> at Nippur and that his son Aga made the Tummal pre-eminent.</p><p>A version of the King List was once read as saying that Dumuzid the Fisherman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> captured Enmebaragesi, but a newer translation reverses this: Enmebaragesi captured Dumuzid, subjugated Uruk and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>-king. Later literature tells it differently. The poem Gilgamesh and Aga has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aga-of-kish">Aga of Kish</a> besiege Uruk, and a praise poem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> credits Gilgamesh with defeating Enmebaragesi himself. One suggestion is that Enmebaragesi was put in the place of Aga because defeating the powerful founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>'s hegemony made Gilgamesh seem mightier.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enmebaragesi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the earliest records Elam appears as a target of Sumerian raids: the Sumerian King List credits King <b>Enmebaragesi</b> of Kish with a victory over the Elamites, traditionally dated to around 2700 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Whether Gilgamesh himself lived is uncertain, but the discovery of artifacts (c. 2600 BCE) associated with <b>Enmebaragesi</b> of Kish, named in the legends as the father of one of Gilgamesh's adversaries, has lent credibility to his historical existence.</blockquote><a href="/work/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Enmebaragesi</b>, who ruled around 2600 BCE, is the earliest king in the list confirmed by his own inscriptions.</blockquote><a href="/state/kish">Kish</a></li></ul></section>
